lisa-addressed:
Human review round 7 (gusfcarvalho, PR #456) — 1 finding, valid, fixed.

- [Medium] ensureProvidedUUIDNotAlreadyInherited (added in round 6) called byComponentIDsForSSP per
  subscribe item: ~4 queries each, re-deriving the SSP's whole requirement/statement/by-component id
  sets from scratch and passing them back as unbounded `IN ?` lists — inside the advisory lock's
  critical section, so it lengthened it too. Tail risk was the sharper half: `IN ?` binds one
  parameter per element against the 65535 ceiling, which is exactly the hazard this same commit
  reasons about for allowedDownstreamSQL and that round 5 worked around with findByIDsInChunks. The
  path just did not follow its own established precedent.
  FIXED: the helper now walks control_implementations -> implemented_requirements -> statements ->
  by_components -> inherited_control_implementations in ONE correlated query with two bind
  parameters, regardless of SSP size. No CAST (ByComponent.ParentID and
  Statement.ImplementedRequirementId are both uuid, and casting to uuid breaks the sqlite unit
  suites); parent_type literals match the previous walker's. Both creation paths still share the one
  helper, so the invariant stays enforced in a single place.
  Kept the call INSIDE the item loop as requested.

  CORRECTION TO THE FINDING'S PREMISE, worth recording: the reviewer asked for a test pinning "two
  items in one subscribe carrying the same provided_uuid", on the reasoning that per-item evaluation
  is what makes the intra-request case work. It turns out that case never reaches the item loop —
  Subscribe already rejects it at request level with 400 ("duplicate provided-uuid %q in request
  items", the seenProvidedUUIDs check, ssp_leverage.go:637). That guard predates the per-SSP
  invariant and exists for its own reason: a repeated provided-uuid would slip past the
  existing-link pre-check (committed rows only) and hit UNIQUE(downstream_ssp_id, provided_uuid)
  mid-transaction. So the per-item placement is defence in depth, not the only thing standing
  between that input and a duplicate. I discovered this by writing the test the reviewer asked for
  and watching it fail with 400 instead of 409.
  Test added anyway as TestSubscribeRejectsDuplicateProvidedUUIDWithinOneRequest, asserting the
  ACTUAL behaviour (400 + the request-level message) and documenting which guard catches it, so the
  next reader is not left with the same wrong assumption. If the request-level guard is ever
  relaxed, that test goes red first and names the reason.

- byComponentIDsForSSP lost its only production caller and golangci-lint flagged it unused. MOVED to
  ssp_leverage_test.go (its two remaining callers are both tests) rather than kept as dead
  production code or silenced with a lint exclusion. Its doc now says it is test-only and why the
  production path stopped materializing the id set.

VERIFICATION: go build clean; golangci-lint 0 issues; go vet -tags integration clean; full unit
suite green; FULL integration suite green (oscal 152s, all other packages ok).
